From d048356812be8118666271b34d017aba03e252ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Remora <remora@localhost>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:36:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] venus: publish display frames on completion, not
 submission
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

vkQueueSubmit RETURNING means the GPU has been asked to render the frame, not
that it has. Publishing there hands the encoder a buffer the compositor may
still be writing into — a real race, and the last correctness gap in the frame
path.

A display frame is now staged at submit and published only once a fence proves
the GPU finished. The fence is armed with QueueSubmit(queue, 0, NULL, fence): an
empty batch whose signal operation, by Vulkan's submission-order rule, completes
only after everything previously submitted on that queue. That is the same idiom
vkr_queue_sync_submit() already uses, so the guarantee being relied on is one
Venus itself depends on.

Venus's OWN queue syncs were tried first and rejected on measurement. They are
submitted roughly per display frame (247 seen against 240 frames) but they
accumulate undrained — 0 retires, and the list never empty — because the guest's
progress does not depend on them here. Waiting on those would have published
nothing at all.

ONE fence, newest frame wins: if the GPU is still finishing frame A when B is
submitted, B replaces A as the next thing to publish. That keeps this to a
single fence and a single wait, and dropping an intermediate frame is the trade
MSG_DONTWAIT already makes on the publish path.

Measured over a live session: 600 published complete, 600 waits, 0 timeouts and
0 coalesced — the fence signals faster than frames arrive, so completion-gating
cost no frames and no throughput. 201 frames imported and 200 encoded in the
same run, decoding clean.

The fallback is deliberately loud: if a fence can never be armed, staging alone
would publish nothing and the mirror would look broken for a subtle reason, so
it reverts to submit-time publishing and says so.
